{"id":"bid-pairs-taking-turns","tier":"REDFLAG","title":"Pairs of bidders that keep meeting: who is lowest, and how close is the other","question":"Among firms that bid against each other again and again, does any pair share the wins between them with the loser's price sitting just above the winner's?","finding":"23 pairs of firms met on six or more of the 92 contested bid sheets. The pair that most often took the low bid between them is Esquivel Grading and Paving, Inc and Ronan Construction, Inc.: one of them was lowest on 9 of the 10 sheets they both entered (5 and 4), where their records on other sheets would predict about 5.0; the other was second on 4, and the typical gap between them was 13%. 1 pair has two or more sheets where the loser was within 2% of the winner. No pair shows the pattern of a cover bid, a strict alternation with the loser a few percent above. 26 firms bid at two or more of the three agencies.","how":"Each published tabulation is one sheet (the grouping and the bidder name key of the bid screens in the site’s “Earmarks, bids and other public bodies” page). For every pair that met on six or more sheets: how often each was the lowest tabulated price, how often the other was second, the gap between the two when one of them was lowest, and the order of wins in time (A, B, or a dash for a third firm). \"Expected\" applies each firm's share of low bids on the sheets where it did not meet the other.","limits":"Street-repaving and sewer work in San Francisco is bid by about half a dozen firms, so the same pairs meet often and one of two strong firms is usually lowest. Sheets are from Public Works since 2022, the Port since 2018 and the transit agency 2015-2022 only; job-order contracts bid as factors are left out. Lowest tabulated price is before the local-business discount and before any bid is withdrawn or rejected. Twenty-odd pairs is too few for a statistical test.","columns":[{"k":"a","t":"Firm A","type":"org"},{"k":"b","t":"Firm B","type":"o